\section{Introduction}
Tring (ISO 639-3 tgq; Glottocode trin1273) is an Austronesian language of Borneo spoken in Sarawak, Malaysia. Sources give very different speaker numbers: Wikipedia cites 550 native speakers (2000), and Joshua Project gives about 2,100 people. It should not be confused with Tringgus (trx), a separate Bidayuh language.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primers, textbooks or school programmes for Tring turned up in the searches. Tring is not known to be taught in schools.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia overview of the Tring language: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tring_language}
\item Glottolog entry for Tring: \url{https://glottolog.org/resource/languoid/id/trin1273.bigmap.html}
\item ASJP word list entry for Tring: \url{https://asjp.clld.org/languages/TRING}
\item The search summaries mention unpublished written materials on Tring in Robert Blust's collection at Kaipuleohone, and Sidney Ray's 1913 \emph{The Languages of Borneo}. I did not see a URL for either, so they are not listed as resources.
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Joshua Project profile of the Tring people: \url{https://joshuaproject.net/people_groups/19545}
\item Joshua Project language page for tgq: \url{https://joshuaproject.net/languages/tgq}
\end{itemize}
No Tring scripture or community organisation was found.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
No audio, video, corpus or software resources for Tring were found.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Very little exists for Tring. A curriculum would have to start with new documentation, working with the community and building on Blust's unpublished materials. The classification and speaker figures also need checking.
